00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.699 align:start position:0%
 
the one thing that wasn't expected  happened US airlines are closely  following the investigation into the  crash of a passenger jet off Indonesia  today the plane was a new Boeing 737 max  out of millions of things they tested  for we unfortunately found one that  wasn't breaking news out of Ethiopia  where a plane went down just outside the  capital killing everyone on board the  Boeing 737 max 8 is the same model  involved in the Indonesian lion air jet  crash in October no aircraft  manufacturer gets a free disaster or two  disasters when these crashes are  preventable by long-established  aerodynamic engineering standards the  crisis surrounding the crashes of the  Boeing 737 max eight planes in Ethiopia  and Indonesia has seized the world's  attention and done something perhaps  even more remarkable unite Congress do  you think the FAA has the responsibility  to tell these airlines that they cannot  shut change safety for profits as a  passenger  I would certainly find it troubling if  the captain is describing the training  manual as quote inadequate and almost  criminally insufficient this is a recent  congressional hearing during which  senators from both parties grilled this  man dan Elwell the Acting Federal  Aviation Administration chief about the  recent Boeing crashes safety is at the  core of the FAS mission it is our first  priority I will testify about a wide  range of issues but the most serious  questions concerned the FAA relationship  with the industry it regulates I suppose  that there's always a question of why we  would have a private company certify  their product and that's something many  Americans might not know or appreciate  the federal agency in charge of making  sure new planes are safe doesn't  actually do those inspections anymore  instead it outsources certifications to  the plane manufacturers themselves the  US government is is not arm's length  with these manufacturers there's too  much delegation of public responsibility  for airline travel safety you might  recognize this man  Ralph Nader is a former Green Party  presidential candidate and prominent  consumer advocate Nader is why we have  seatbelts and airbags and cars  he's also been sounding the alarm about  what he sees as the cozy relationship  between the FAA and aircraft  manufacturers like Boeing for years he  even wrote a book about it in 1994 it's  a captive agency it's captive by the  airline industry and the aircraft  manufacturers who swarm over it who get  their executives appointed as officials  of the FAA so this book is designed to  encourage an attitude of prevention of  foreseen for stalling why wait for the  crashes to occur  recently the issue became more personal  for Nader his grandniece Sammy Rose Stu  Moe was killed in the 737 max crash in  Ethiopia  she was only 24 but in her short life  she had an advanced degree in global  health we'll never know how many lives  she would have saved in a 50-year career  we can trace the roots of the modern FAA  back to a major change the agency  instituted in 2005 back then the Bush  administration loosened regulations to  allow manufacturers to use their own  employees to certify the safety of their  planes this was part of a cost-cutting  initiative dubbed Oda or the  organization designation authorization  program this cushy relationship between  the FAA and Boeing has got to end  and it congress has got to stand up  provide adequate budgets and adequate  technical staff to do their job in a  statement the FAA said the agency has  never allowed companies to police  themselves or self-certify their  aircraft before the ODA came into effect  the FAA would choose its own inspectors  some of those inspectors may have worked  for the manufacturer but they were  chosen and supervised by the FAA today  under the ODA system Boeing employees  certify the safety of Boeing's own  planes grenadier and the agency's  critics this is a clear conflict of  interest but for the FAA is defenders  these concerns are overblown the  certification process the FAA is  involved from start to finish if you  went out to Boeing or Airbus or anywhere  where aircraft certification was  underway you would find FAA pilots  engineers all part of the process  working shoulder-to-shoulder I don't  think you could operate the airline  industry or the commercial aircraft  industry without it and that's something  both sides can agree on today the FAA  simply cannot do all of these  inspections because it doesn't have the  manpower or the resources if we had no  Oda and it would require roughly 10,000  more employees in about a 1.8 billion  dollars for our certification office in  the FAA  this would be a major increase from the  FAS 2019 budget of 239 million dollars  and 1,300 staff we would also represent  a significant change to the agency's  marching orders considering that for the  past 15 years Congress has asked the FAA  to tighten its belt and deregulate every  decade or so this catches the attention  of folks who are  in the aviation business Congress the  press you're cranky uncle they scratch  their heads what do you mean the  government is working with the airlines  aren't they supposed to be regulating  those guys this is a speech that Elwell  gave last year to an industry lobbying  group I get it  I get that thinking that thought process  but the relationship that exists between  the FAA  ALPA and the rest of the industry that  we regulate is the driving force behind  our unprecedented safety record and that  is perhaps the FAA is best defense  despite these recent crashes modern air  safety for large transport aircraft in  America is better than ever since 1997  the risk of a fatal commercial aviation  accident in the United States has been  cut by 94% and in the past 10 years  we've experienced one commercial airline  paced passenger fatality in the United  States in over 90 million flights that's  a remarkable record so then what  happened with these two flights since  both investigations are still ongoing  it's too early to say but former FAA  head Babbitt thinks the answer is an  uncomfortable one I'm not trying to be  defensive of the FAA but I would imagine  there's probably more than a hundred  thousand tests were done on this  aircraft and to have a shortcoming in  one that's one shortcoming too many but  out of the mass of literally and  millions of permutations of what could  happen you know what the goal line--  every now and then the holes and the  Swiss cheese line up and that's what  happened  but the agency's critics see things very  differently for them the two recent  crashes are further proof that  deregulation has gotten out of control  and that industry has effectively taken  over the FAA for Nader  a case in point is the men at the top  dan Elwell  well that was part of the industry FAA  turnstile they go in they go out the  current acting FAA had has spent his  career bouncing between government and  industry fortunately he's on his way out  is gonna be a new FAA commissioner the  problem is he's from Delta so here we go  again Boeing is determined to salvage  the 737 max 8 which is the fastest  selling plane in the company's history  to that end they are working on a  software fix that they hope will get the  max aids back in the air flying again  this summer but the growing public  scrutiny of the manufacturer could  undermine that deadline and perhaps even  Boeing's influence within the FAA a  recent Wall Street Journal investigation  found that Boeing didn't share  information about a problem with a  cockpit safety alert system for about a  year before the first max crash in  Indonesia that left not only the flying  public in the dark but also the FAA it  was only after the second accident in  Ethiopia nearly five months later that  Boeing became more forthcoming about the  problem  Boeing said it operates in full  accordance with all Federal Aviation  Administration oversight requirements  and processes the long-standing  collaborative engagement between the FAA  Boeing its customers and industry  partners has created the safest  transportation system in the world  [Music]

